% ---- Section 1: Introduction ----
\section{Introduction}

Policymakers as well as members of the general public have long been 
concerned with the \mbox{housing} market. In recent years, Madrid, the capital city of 
Spain, has experienced a sharp rise in real estate values, positioning it among 
the most expensive housing markets in the country. This upward trend has 
sparked intense debate, not only because of its economic significance, but also 
because of its social implications, particularly in terms of affordability and 
accessibility for young people and families.

\vspace{0.15cm}

Identifying the main determinants of housing prices is therefore of great 
interest. Several attributes are potential candidates: the surface area of the 
dwelling, the number of rooms, the presence of amenities such as elevators, 
garages or terraces, and, perhaps most importantly, the location of the 
property. By studying these characteristics, we aim to assess which factors are 
most strongly associated with price differences across districts of Madrid.

\vspace{0.15cm}

The primary objective of this project is to provide a descriptive exploration 
of real estate data collected from the \emph{Idealista} platform. Using this 
dataset, we will summarize and visualize the main features of the Madrid 
housing market, and test simple hypotheses regarding the effect of surface 
area, location, and property characteristics on price. This preliminary work 
will serve as the foundation for more advanced statistical modelling in later 
stages of the project.

\vspace{0.15cm}

To be more precise, our main research question is formulated as follows:
\begin{quote}
\textit{What are the key determinants of housing prices in Madrid, and how do 
property characteristics and location explain the variation in price per square 
meter?}
\end{quote}

% ---- Section 2: Dataset ----
\section{Dataset}

The dataset used in this project was obtained from the \emph{Idealista} 
platform\footnote{\url{https://www.idealista.com}} and contains information on 
real estate listings in Madrid. It includes several quantitative and 
categorical variables describing both the price and the main characteristics of 
the properties. Since the data were originally collected from online listings, 
a careful curation process was necessary to ensure consistency and to remove 
invalid entries (e.g., duplicated records, missing or unrealistic values of 
price or surface).

\vspace{0.1cm}

Below we load the dataset and display the variable structure:

<<>>=
data <- read.csv("idealista.csv", header=TRUE, sep=",", stringsAsFactors=TRUE)

for (i in seq_along(data)) {
  var_name <- names(data)[i]
  var_type <- class(data[[i]])[1]
  cat(paste0("- ", var_name, ": ", var_type, "\n"))
}
@

\textit{Note: Instead of using the \texttt{str()} function, we manually display 
the names and types of the variables to avoid compilation issues with long R 
outputs in the \texttt{Sweave} + \texttt{pdflatex} pipeline.}

\vspace{0.1cm}

For the purposes of our analysis, we curated the dataset and focused on the 
following key variables of interest:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Price}: Advertised price of the dwelling (in euros). Used as a 
  primary dependent variable.
  
  \item \textbf{Surface}: Surface area of the property in square meters. A key 
  quantitative predictor.
  
  \item \textbf{Price per m$^2$}: Derived variable defined as 
  \(\texttt{Price} / \texttt{Surface}\), used as the main response variable in 
  subsequent analysis.
  
  \item \textbf{District}: Administrative district of Madrid where the 
  dwelling is located. Categorical variable.
  
  \item \textbf{Rooms}: Number of rooms in the dwelling (integer).
  
  \item \textbf{Elevator}: Dummy variable (1 if the building has elevator, 0 
  otherwise).
  
  \item \textbf{Garage}: Dummy variable indicating the presence of a garage.
  
  \item \textbf{Terrace}: Dummy variable indicating the presence of a terrace.
\end{itemize}

Additional variables present in the raw dataset (such as textual descriptions 
or incomplete fields) were excluded during the curation step, as they were not 
relevant for the scope of this descriptive analysis. To ensure consistency, we 
restricted our dataset to complete observations of the variables of interest 
(price, surface, rooms, district, elevator, garage, terrace), and created a 
derived measure of price per square meter. The final curated dataset is therefore 
cleaner and well-suited for descriptive and exploratory statistics.

\vspace{0.2cm}

<<curate, echo=TRUE,results='hide'>>=
# Rename relevant columns to consistent English names
df <- data.frame(
  price = data$PrecioActual,
  surface = data$metros,
  rooms = data$habitaciones,
  district = data$zona,
  elevator = data$ascensor,
  garage = data$tags,     
  terrace = data$tags
)

# Keep only complete rows
df <- na.omit(df)

# Remove possible zero or negative surfaces
df <- subset(df, surface > 0)

# Create derived variable
df$price_m2 <- df$price / df$surface

@

% ---- Section 3: Hypothesis to analyse ----
\section{Hypothesis to analyse}

Our analysis focuses on identifying the most influential factor behind housing 
prices in Madrid. In particular, we contrast the role of location (district) 
with other property characteristics.

\begin{itemize}
  \item $H_0$: Location is the main determinant of price per square meter in 
  Madrid, outweighing the effect of other housing features.  
  \item $H_1$: Other property characteristics (surface, rooms, elevator, garage, 
  terrace) explain more variation in price per square meter than location.  
\end{itemize}

% ---- Section 4: Descriptive Statistics ----
\section{Descriptive Statistics}

We provide below a basic statistical summary of the curated dataset, focusing 
on the main quantitative variables: \texttt{price}, \texttt{surface}, 
\texttt{rooms}, and the derived \texttt{price\_m2}. These statistics allow us 
to gain a first impression of the central tendencies and dispersion of housing 
values across Madrid.

<<summary, echo=TRUE>>=
summary(df[, c("price", "surface", "rooms", "price_m2")])
@

\vspace{0.2cm}

To complement the numerical summaries, we also explore the distribution of 
listings across districts and visualize some key relationships between 
variables.

<<graphics, echo=FALSE>>=
# Multi-panel layout
par(mfrow = c(2, 2))  # 2 rows, 2 columns

# Histogram of price per m²
par(mar=c(5, 5, 4, 2))
hist(df$price_m2, breaks=50, col="lightgreen",
     main="Distribution of Price per m²", 
     xlab="Price per m² (euros)", 
     ylab="Number of Properties")

# Scatterplot price vs surface
par(mar=c(5, 5, 4, 2))
plot(df$surface, df$price, pch=19, col=rgb(0,0,1,0.3),
     main="Relation between Surface and Price", 
     xlab="Surface Area (m²)", 
     ylab="Total Price (euros)",
     xlim=c(0,3100))

# --- Fix district labels ---
df$district <- as.character(df$district)
df$district <- gsub("barrio-de-salamanca", "Salamanca", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("villa-de-vallecas", "Vallecas", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("ciudad-lineal", "Ciudad Lineal", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("san-blas", "San Blas", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("arganzuela", "Arganzuela", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("usera", "Usera", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("moratalaz", "Moratalaz", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("vicalvaro", "Vicálvaro", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("carabanchel", "Carabanchel", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("tetuan", "Tetuán", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("hortaleza", "Hortaleza", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("moncloa", "Moncloa", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("chamberi", "Chamberí", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("centro", "Centro", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("retiro", "Retiro", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("latina", "Latina", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("fuencarral", "Fuencarral", df$district)
df$district <- gsub("puente-de-vallecas", "Pte. de Vallecas", df$district)


# Barplot average price per m² by district
par(mar=c(8, 5, 4, 2))
barplot(sort(tapply(df$price_m2, df$district, mean, na.rm=TRUE), decreasing=TRUE),
        col="salmon", las=2,
        main="Average Price (euros) per m² by District")

# Barplot number of listings by district
par(mar=c(8, 5, 4, 2))
barplot(table(df$district), las=2, col="lightblue",
        main="Number of Listings per District")

# Reset to default
par(mfrow = c(1, 1), mar=c(5, 4, 4, 2) + 0.1)
@

